<Project Sdk="BHS.Revit.Sdk/1.5.8">

    <!--
      What the application has to know about cabling before anybody presses anything.

      A class of Revit registration can only be made while Revit is starting - FailureDefinition
      says so in its own documentation - and is wanted later, in a command Revit constructs from a
      string. Until this project existed there was nowhere to put the identifiers those two halves
      have to agree on: the feature assemblies load on a press, which is too late, and the framework
      is not allowed to know what a cable is.

      NOT ONLY IDENTIFIERS. NeedsProjectDocument lives here too: the availability rule the cabling
      buttons ask, which is a decision that executes, and executes before anybody presses anything -
      Revit asks it whenever the tab holding the buttons is shown. It belongs here for the same reason
      the identifiers do, and it stays inside the rule below by reading nothing but the context Revit
      passes: no collector, no settings, no services. The class Revit constructs for it is an empty
      name in BHS.MEP.Cabling.Entry, because Revit resolves an availability class only inside the
      assembly its button names.

      THE ONE RULE, AND IT IS ENFORCED RATHER THAN REMEMBERED: this assembly may not reference the
      implementation - not .Feature, not .Revit, not .Ui, not .Routing. It is loaded during
      OnStartup for every user of every edition, whether or not the feature is ever used, so a
      reference here would undo the laziness the ribbon manifest exists to protect. Without the
      reference, the mistake cannot be typed: a `typeof` naming a command does not compile.
      RVTDEC001 in RefCheck checks the built assembly, because a rule held by memory is a rule this
      repository has already watched lapse.

      No RevitAPIUI either, and that is the second half of being a declaration: FailureDefinition
      lives in RevitAPI, and so do the Document and CategorySet an availability rule is shown, so
      nothing here needs an interface type, and a DBApplication edition can register exactly the same
      things. RVTDEC002. The interface half of availability - IExternalCommandAvailability and
      UIApplication - is the framework's AvailabilityEntryPoint, which this assembly never names.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFrameworks>net48-revit2024;net8.0-revit2025;net8.0-revit2026;net10.0-revit2027</TargetFrameworks>
        <RootNamespace>BHS.MEP.Cabling.Declaration</RootNamespace>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Revit\BHS.Revit.Abstractions\BHS.Revit.Abstractions.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
